{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts a 'shared mechanism' relation between SRC_002 and SRC_004, but the refutation clause is narrowly tailored to an async-routing-phase incompatibility and a program-eval absence, neither of which actually tests whether the mechanism is genuinely shared. The substantive problem asks for a relation that connects the isolated artifact to its neighbourhood; here, naming 'typed-contract enforcement' as a shared mechanism without specifying the overlapping enforcement step (e.g., which program-eval branch both artifacts exercise) reduces the relation to a label. The refutation-if clause does not fire on this deficiency: SRC_004 could require an async routing phase and SRC_002 could still share the same typed-contract label superficially while the underlying eval paths diverge entirely. A genuine shared-mechanism claim must identify the common enforcement operation, not merely assert both use program eval for a forbidden case — every program-eval artifact does that trivially.","counterexample":["If SRC_002 enforces typed-contract fidelity via a synchronous exception-code branch while SRC_004 enforces it via an async validation-timing branch, the 'shared mechanism' label holds superficially but the two share no enforcement operation; the refutation clause (requiring async routing phase OR program-eval absence) would not fire, yet the shared-mechanism claim is substantively vacuous."],"target":"340a36c7c283f0903b671b79dfd11baa1dbab778081e43c05180dae3049ad66b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 asserts a 'contradiction' relation between SRC_004 and SRC_005, but the refutation clause's second disjunct ('SRC_004's counterconditions overlap with SRC_005's synchronous precondition rather than negating it') reveals the relation is not properly established: the clause only refutes if overlap is found, yet it does not require the target to demonstrate actual negation of the precondition. The contradiction claim rests on temporal ordering (post-call vs pre-call), but a pre-call lease-mismatch refusal and a post-call mutation detection are compatible — SRC_005 can refuse before the provider call AND SRC_004 can detect a different mutation after the provider call under the same fixture, addressing different contract layers. The target labels this 'contradiction' without ruling out the layer-separation compatibility, and the refutation-if clause's first disjunct (firewall enforcing both under the same fixture without invoking the provider) actually describes a scenario where the contradiction dissolves, yet is listed as a refutation condition rather than a reason the base relation is already unsupported.","counterexample":["A firewall that performs a synchronous pre-call lease check (SRC_005) and then, after a no-op stub returns, detects a post-call mutation (SRC_004) under the same frozen fixture — both hold simultaneously, making 'contradiction' the wrong relation; 'compatibility' or 'integration' across contract layers would be more accurate."],"target":"5b3400345fc1446c722fd97bd918166e4f3f48373631f84e1546d3acb9f3eda3"}]}